  "query_preview": "..\nWidespread smoke remains across the region this afternoon, with\na dry northwest flow pattern emerging behind the departed upper\ntrough. High pressure then continues to build over the area\nthrough midweek. This will maintain warmer daytime temperatures\nand low relative humidity values each day. With winds generally\nfrom the north/northwest, expect additional rounds of smoke to\nimpact the region from local and regional fires. Once the smoke\narrives, easing winds and stronger high pressure will leave\nlingering issues. Air Quality Alerts have been issued by air\nquality agencies across central and eastern Washington, as well\nas north Idaho. While the current temperature forecast continues \nto reflect temperatures reaching well into the 90s Wednesday\nthrough Friday, the impact of the widespread smoke across the\nregion could hold daytime temperatures a little cooler than\nwould otherwise be expected.\n\nLooking ahead to the end of the week and into the weekend, dry\nair remains in place but the high pressure ridge begins to\nflatten out. This zonal flow and the dry air mass will bring\nincreased potential for wind gusts around 30 mph through the\nCascade gaps and around 20 mph across portions of eastern\nWashington Friday and Saturday. This will bring the potential\nfor elevated to locally critical fire weather conditions.",
